---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro'
import CityExplorer from '../components/CityExplorer.astro'
import { cities, cityOrder, ratio, AFFORDABLE_CUTOFF, ratioBarPercent } from '../data/cities'

const rows = cityOrder.map((k) => ({ name: cities[k].name, value: ratio(cities[k]) }))
const maxRatio = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.value))
---
<ChapterLayout
  num="02"
  slug="cities"
  title="The Cities"
  sub="Where ambition goes to pay rent."
  description="A sourced 2024 Q3 metropolitan housing-affordability comparison for Toronto, Vancouver, Greater London and New York."
  readingTime="~5 min + interactive"
  sources={['demographia_2025', 'nyc_comptroller_2024', 'gla_2024']}
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <p class="prose-faust text-xl leading-relaxed text-surveillance/80 dark:text-cream/80">
      Ambition does not float free. It is priced. In the metropolitan markets where this story
      concentrates, the cost of a stable life is high relative to the incomes meant to support
      it. In the 2024 third-quarter snapshot, the median home-price-to-gross-household-income
      multiples range from <strong>7.4 to 11.8</strong> — compared with Demographia's
      “affordable” category cutoff of <strong>3.0</strong>. Extreme effort can start to look
      less like greed and more like a response to economic pressure. That last link is an
      interpretation, not a causal result established by these figures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <CityExplorer />

  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between mb-8">
      <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl md:text-3xl">Metropolitan median multiples, compared</h2>
      <span class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-surveillance/40 dark:text-cream/40">Fig 02.1</span>
    </div>
    <div class="surface p-6 md:p-10">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <div>
              <div class="flex justify-between font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">
                <span>{r.name}</span>
                <span class="text-rust font-bold">{r.value.toFixed(1)}x</span>
              </div>
              <div class="h-6 bg-surveillance/5 dark:bg-cream/5 relative">
                <div class="h-full bg-rust" style={`width:${ratioBarPercent(r.value, maxRatio)}%`}></div>
                <div
                  class="absolute top-0 bottom-0 border-l-2 border-dashed border-soft-green"
                  style={`left:${ratioBarPercent(AFFORDABLE_CUTOFF, maxRatio)}%`}
                  aria-hidden="true"
                ></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-soft-green mt-6">
        Dashed mark: Demographia “affordable” cutoff (≤ 3.0x), not an observed market
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-surveillance/60 dark:text-cream/60 mt-3">
      Source: Wendell Cox, <a class="text-rust underline" href="https://www.chapman.edu/communication/_files/Demographia-International-Housing-Affordability-2025-Edition.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Demographia 2025</a>,
      Tables 3–4 (printed pp. 22–25); figures measure 2024 Q3. Median multiples divide median
      home price by gross median household income within each metropolitan market. The source
      publishes rounded multiples, not the underlying monetary pairs. Separate burden estimates
      shown above use different populations and must not be read as comparable ownership ratios.
    </p>
  </section>
</ChapterLayout>
